Q: How can I stop ReactGridLayout elements from resizing when the containing window is resized? A: <p>The issue is that <code>WidthProvider</code> inherently makes the grid fluid by dividing the current container width by your <code>cols</code> count to determine individual column widths. When you dynamically alter <code>cols</code> and <code>w</code> simultaneously via a window multiplier, the math still stretches the elements because <code>react-grid-layout</code> re-calculates column pixels dynamically on window resize.</p>
<p>To maintain a strict <strong>fixed pixel width</strong> for elements on window resize while allowing users to manually move and resize them across an expanded space (like dual monitors), you need to drop <code>WidthProvider</code> and manually pass a fixed, large container <code>width</code> to the base <code>GridLayout</code>.</p>
<p>Here is the cleanest way to fix your component using a fixed pixel grid logic:</p>
<pre><code>import React, { useState, useEffect } from &quot;react&quot;;
import GridLayout from &quot;react-grid-layout&quot;;
import GridElement1 from './GridElement1.jsx';
import GridElement2 from './GridElement2.jsx';

function Workspace() {
  // Define a constant scale: e.g., each column represents exactly 1 pixel
  // Or make 1 col = 10px to keep layout arrays lightweight.
  const COL_WIDTH_PX = 1; 
  const FIXED_MAX_WIDTH = 5000; // Large enough to span across multiple monitors easily

  return (
    &lt;div className=&quot;workspace-container&quot; style={{ width: &quot;100%&quot;, overflowX: &quot;auto&quot; }}&gt;
      &lt;GridLayout
        className=&quot;workspace&quot;
        cols={FIXED_MAX_WIDTH}
        rowHeight={30}
        containerPadding={[0, 0]}
        margin={[10, 10]}
        // By leaving width static and large, elements preserve their explicit coordinate mapping size
        width={FIXED_MAX_WIDTH * COL_WIDTH_PX} 
        autoSize={true}
      &gt;
        &lt;div
          key=&quot;a&quot;
          data-grid={{
            x: 0,
            y: 0,
            w: 460, // Strictly 460 columns wide (Static 460px size)
            h: 9,
            minH: 2,
            maxH: 20,
            isResizable: true,
            resizeHandles: [&quot;s&quot;]
          }}
        &gt;
          &lt;GridElement1/&gt;
        &lt;/div&gt;
        &lt;div
          key=&quot;b&quot;
          data-grid={{
            x: 480, // Giving some layout offset padding manually
            y: 0,
            w: 550, // Strictly 550 columns wide
            h: 9,
            minH: 2,
            maxH: 20,
            isResizable: true,
            resizeHandles: [&quot;s&quot;]
          }}
        &gt;
          &lt;GridElement2/&gt;
        &lt;/div&gt;
      &lt;/GridLayout&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
  );
}

export default Workspace;
</code></pre>
<p>Why this works perfectly:</p>
<ol>
<li><p><strong>Removed <code>WidthProvider</code></strong>: This stops <code>react-grid-layout</code> from listening to immediate browser resize actions and forcing element expansions.</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>Static <code>width</code> attribute</strong>: Passing a large, static <code>width</code> to <code>&lt;GridLayout&gt;</code> ensures your mathematical grid boundaries never compress or dilate when you stretch the parent window across monitors.</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>Prop naming correction</strong>: Changed <code>dataGrid</code> to the officially supported <code>data-grid</code> prop to prevent hidden layout rendering bugs.</p>
</li>
</ol>

Q: How to replace all instances of a placeholder value within a dictionary&#39;s values? A: <p>A simple recursion would do the trick</p>
<pre><code>def replace_env(obj, env: str):
    if isinstance(obj, dict):
        return {replace_env(k, env): replace_env(v, env) for k, v in obj.items()}
    if isinstance(obj, list):
        return [replace_env(x, env) for x in obj]
    if isinstance(obj, str):
        return obj.replace(&quot;{env}&quot;, env)
    return obj


if __name__ == &quot;__main__&quot;:
    my_dct = {
        &quot;pipeline&quot;: &quot;Test Pipeline&quot;,
        &quot;environment&quot;: &quot;dev&quot;,
        &quot;test&quot;: {
            &quot;{env}_test2&quot;: {
                &quot;test3&quot;: [&quot;{env}_test4&quot;, &quot;{env}_test5&quot;],
            }
        },
    }
    print(replace_env(my_dct, &quot;dev&quot;))
</code></pre>
